@@ -34,6 +34,7 @@ Sagittarius 1.0.11 is the current stable research SDK release. This page records
 - Current MCWF `connected_pair_correlation` averages per-trajectory covariance, generally different from covariance constructed from ensemble pair/population means. The observable campaign uses bitstring decay controls where per-trajectory covariance is zero; no general nonlinear ensemble-equivalence claim is established.
 - Phase 16 `ParallelSimulation` sweep evidence uses local Julia `Distributed` workers and a deterministic parameter-map kernel. It does not validate multi-node scheduling, MPI/Slurm deployment, or end-to-end physics-solver sweep scalability.
 - `experiment-config/v1` reproduces JSON-declared register, pulse, solver, observable, readout, and basis-state inputs only. Python callable pulses, arbitrary complex initial-state vectors, named helper forms, parameter sweeps, and hardware-calibration inputs remain outside this contract. The Python SDK provides all-ground, bitstring, and single-excitation helpers for direct simulation calls; uniform-superposition preparation remains unsupported.
+- MCWF results have no final-state sampling payload. Their current manifest readout metadata enumerates nominal full-space bitstrings even for reduced execution; this is not the executed basis or a final probability distribution. Use the solver radius and initial-state basis size for execution provenance. This nominal enumeration also adds full-space metadata memory/storage cost to reduced MCWF calls.
 - Raw MCWF trajectory retention scales with observable count, output time samples, and trajectory count. It is opt-in diagnostic data, so plan memory and artifact capacity before enabling it for large ensembles.
 - Stored samples require a common finite time axis and the same trajectory count for every observable. `trajectory-data/v1` rejects mismatched shapes, time values, or observable ordering during save/load. Raw samples are intentionally absent from `shared-result/v1`.
 
